"The patient underwent a robot-assisted radical prostatectomy with a pathological stage of pT3a, positive surgical margins, and no seminal vesicle invasion or lymph node metastasis. The preoperative PSA was elevated at 27. The surgical findings indicate a Gleason 4+3 with tertiary pattern 5 (ISUP grade group 3). The absence of lymph node metastasis is a favorable factor. Conversely, positive surgical margins and the higher preoperative PSA contribute to increased risk. Biopsy findings showed a Gleason 3+4, which differs from the prostatectomy specimen. Missing postoperative PSA and follow-up data significantly widen the uncertainty regarding the trajectory of recurrence. The current prognostic horizon suggests a time frame for recurrence. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and subsequent changes would be crucial in refining this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 37.26 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 27.94–46.57 months; its coverage has not been validated."
